caseStudy:{title:"Decision committee: $250,000 Connecticut family property financed with a new policy and trust",scenario:"A family can save $300/month and hopes to acquire a $250,000 home, leave it for a daughter and reduce taxes. A promoter offers permanent life insurance, a loan on future policy value, and an irrevocable trust, claiming the family can avoid a down payment, mortgage liability and personal income tax. Compare this with affordable term protection, ordinary savings and reviewed estate documents.",steps:[["House price","$250,000"],["5% down payment need","$12,500 plus closing costs and reserve"],["New permanent policy at $300/month","$3,600 yearly cash expense; no guaranteed immediate $12,500 loan value"],["Policy borrowing","Interest-bearing debt, not net equity created"],["First mortgage","$237,500 before any other liens on 5% down example"],["Trust ownership","Requires valid deed, lender rights and insured records; mortgage remains"],["Trust tax classification","Grantor or nongrantor analysis, not automatic exemption"],["Inheritance basis","Gift versus decedent property may change later capital gain"],["CT estate tax","2026 $15 million exemption is separate from family liquidity planning"],["Adverse case","10% home decline, reduced income, rising premium, policy loan interest"],["Independent verification","Tax professional, estate attorney, lender and insurer documents"]],
decision:"Write a final comprehensive committee memo with three alternatives, complete balance-sheet accounting, cash after closing, insurer value and loan contract, credible income-tax and estate-tax treatment, beneficiary support and a documented adverse-case failure plan."}
